1
00:00:02,833 --> 00:00:04,500
The most famous trip home in history

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:05,733
took about ten years.

3
00:00:06,067 --> 00:00:08,233
He spent eight of them as a houseguest:

4
00:00:08,233 --> 00:00:10,267
seven years with the nymph Calypso,

5
00:00:10,267 --> 00:00:11,667
one with the witch Circe.

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:13,600
And when he finally got home,

7
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,000
a hundred and eight houseguests

8
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:15,967
were eating his food.

9
00:00:19,967 --> 00:00:21,933
Hi, and welcome to LearnLoop,

10
00:00:21,933 --> 00:00:24,000
where old stories loop back to your life.

11
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,233
This is Homer's Odyssey,

12
00:00:26,233 --> 00:00:28,300
a poem about 2,700 years old.

13
00:00:28,633 --> 00:00:30,767
Troy has just fallen after ten years of war.

14
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:33,433
Odysseus, king of the island of Ithaca,

15
00:00:33,433 --> 00:00:35,633
sets sail for home with twelve ships.

16
00:00:36,633 --> 00:00:39,167
Waiting at home are his wife, Penelope,

17
00:00:39,167 --> 00:00:41,133
and a son who was a baby when he left.

18
00:00:41,467 --> 00:00:42,500
It should be a quick trip.

19
00:00:42,833 --> 00:00:43,533
It is not.

20
00:00:44,533 --> 00:00:46,767
The monsters come from one dinner party,

21
00:00:46,767 --> 00:00:49,000
where Odysseus tells the story himself.

22
00:00:49,333 --> 00:00:50,533
So this is his version.

23
00:00:51,533 --> 00:00:53,733
Early on, his men walk into the cave

24
00:00:53,733 --> 00:00:56,100
of a giant Cyclops called Polyphemus.

25
00:00:56,433 --> 00:00:57,567
He traps them inside

26
00:00:57,567 --> 00:00:59,633
and eats six of the crew, two at a time.

27
00:00:59,967 --> 00:01:02,533
Odysseus tells the giant his name is Nobody.

28
00:01:02,867 --> 00:01:04,867
That night, while the giant sleeps,

29
00:01:04,867 --> 00:01:06,067
they blind him with a stake

30
00:01:06,067 --> 00:01:07,100
hardened in the fire.

31
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:09,467
He screams for help,

32
00:01:09,467 --> 00:01:11,367
and his neighbors ask who is hurting him.

33
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:12,800
Nobody, he shouts.

34
00:01:13,133 --> 00:01:14,433
So they shrug and go home.

35
00:01:14,767 --> 00:01:16,233
At dawn, the men escape

36
00:01:16,233 --> 00:01:17,600
under the bellies of his rams.

37
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:20,933
A perfect escape, until Odysseus ruins it.

38
00:01:21,267 --> 00:01:23,700
From his ship, he shouts his real name,

39
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:24,733
his father's name,

40
00:01:24,733 --> 00:01:25,733
and his home island.

41
00:01:26,067 --> 00:01:28,300
History's first case of sharing your location

42
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:29,300
with the wrong person.

43
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:32,067
The Cyclops is a son of Poseidon,

44
00:01:32,067 --> 00:01:32,867
god of the sea.

45
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:34,933
He prays to his father:

46
00:01:34,933 --> 00:01:37,233
let Odysseus get home late, alone,

47
00:01:37,233 --> 00:01:38,467
on someone else's ship.

48
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:40,567
Poseidon won't kill Odysseus,

49
00:01:40,567 --> 00:01:42,100
but he'll keep him away from home.

50
00:01:42,433 --> 00:01:44,100
And between here and Ithaca,

51
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:45,433
there is a lot of sea.

52
00:01:47,267 --> 00:01:49,433
From here on, every stop goes wrong.

53
00:01:49,767 --> 00:01:50,733
The keeper of the winds

54
00:01:50,733 --> 00:01:51,567
gives him a bag

55
00:01:51,567 --> 00:01:53,433
with the storm winds tied inside.

56
00:01:53,767 --> 00:01:55,900
For nine days they sail straight home,

57
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:58,367
and with Ithaca in sight, he falls asleep.

58
00:01:59,367 --> 00:02:01,367
His crew thinks the bag is full of gold.

59
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:02,400
They open it,

60
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:04,200
and the winds blow them all the way back.

61
00:02:04,533 --> 00:02:05,467
Recalculating.

62
00:02:06,467 --> 00:02:08,033
Next, a tribe of giants

63
00:02:08,033 --> 00:02:09,667
smashes every ship but his.

64
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:11,367
Twelve ships left Troy.

65
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:12,733
Now there is one.

66
00:02:13,733 --> 00:02:14,833
On the next island,

67
00:02:14,833 --> 00:02:16,567
Circe turns his men into pigs.

68
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:18,333
Pig bodies, human minds.

69
00:02:18,667 --> 00:02:20,133
Hermes gives Odysseus a herb

70
00:02:20,133 --> 00:02:21,200
that blocks her magic.

71
00:02:21,533 --> 00:02:22,200
It works.

72
00:02:22,533 --> 00:02:23,800
He stays a year anyway.

73
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:25,967
Then come the Sirens.

74
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:28,167
In Homer there are just two,

75
00:02:28,167 --> 00:02:30,233
and what they promise is knowledge.

76
00:02:30,567 --> 00:02:32,967
Odysseus plugs his crew's ears with wax

77
00:02:32,967 --> 00:02:34,867
and has himself tied to the mast.

78
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:36,667
Noise-cancelling for the crew,

79
00:02:36,667 --> 00:02:38,200
a live concert for the captain.

80
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:41,233
Next, a strait with two bad options.

81
00:02:41,567 --> 00:02:44,200
Scylla, a monster with six heads,

82
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:46,233
or Charybdis, a whirlpool

83
00:02:46,233 --> 00:02:48,667
that swallows the sea three times a day.

84
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:50,567
Scylla takes six of his men.

85
00:02:51,567 --> 00:02:53,233
On the island of the sun god,

86
00:02:53,233 --> 00:02:55,833
the starving crew eats his sacred cattle.

87
00:02:56,167 --> 00:02:58,500
Zeus sinks the ship with a thunderbolt,

88
00:02:58,500 --> 00:02:59,967
and only Odysseus survives.

89
00:03:00,967 --> 00:03:02,667
He washes up on Calypso's island.

90
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:04,633
She keeps him for seven years,

91
00:03:04,633 --> 00:03:06,367
and offers to make him immortal.

92
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:07,533
He says no.

93
00:03:07,867 --> 00:03:09,100
He wants to go home,

94
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:11,100
where his wife has a problem of her own.

95
00:03:12,933 --> 00:03:14,867
So Odysseus is stuck on one island.

96
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:16,667
Penelope is stuck on another.

97
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,067
A hundred and eight men want to marry her,

98
00:03:19,067 --> 00:03:20,933
and they won't leave until she picks one.

99
00:03:21,933 --> 00:03:24,700
Meanwhile, they party in the palace every day,

100
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:27,100
on his cattle, his sheep and his wine.

101
00:03:27,433 --> 00:03:28,933
They eat everything in sight,

102
00:03:28,933 --> 00:03:31,000
and they'd still give the place one star.

103
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:33,133
Penelope stalls.

104
00:03:33,467 --> 00:03:35,467
She'll choose a husband, she says,

105
00:03:35,467 --> 00:03:37,133
once she finishes weaving a shroud

106
00:03:37,133 --> 00:03:38,433
for Odysseus's father.

107
00:03:38,767 --> 00:03:40,133
She weaves it by day

108
00:03:40,133 --> 00:03:41,933
and secretly unpicks it by night.

109
00:03:42,267 --> 00:03:43,167
For three years.

110
00:03:44,167 --> 00:03:46,333
In the fourth year, a maid gives her away.

111
00:03:46,667 --> 00:03:49,200
Meanwhile, their son, the baby from the start,

112
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:49,833
is grown up.

113
00:03:50,167 --> 00:03:52,533
He sails off to find news of his father,

114
00:03:52,533 --> 00:03:54,433
guided by an old friend called Mentor.

115
00:03:55,433 --> 00:03:57,867
Mentor is really Athena in disguise,

116
00:03:57,867 --> 00:04:00,100
and that's where our word mentor comes from.

117
00:04:00,433 --> 00:04:02,767
The suitors plan to ambush him on his way home.

118
00:04:03,100 --> 00:04:05,233
Now Penelope is out of excuses,

119
00:04:05,233 --> 00:04:06,833
the suitors are out of patience,

120
00:04:06,833 --> 00:04:08,733
and Odysseus is out of ships.

121
00:04:10,567 --> 00:04:13,100
The gods finally order Calypso to let him go.

122
00:04:13,433 --> 00:04:14,967
A kingdom of sailors carries him

123
00:04:14,967 --> 00:04:16,933
the rest of the way, fast asleep.

124
00:04:17,933 --> 00:04:19,400
Twenty years after he left,

125
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:20,533
Odysseus is home.

126
00:04:20,867 --> 00:04:23,000
Late, alone, on someone else's ship.

127
00:04:23,333 --> 00:04:25,233
Exactly what the Cyclops asked for.

128
00:04:25,567 --> 00:04:26,533
You have arrived.

129
00:04:27,533 --> 00:04:29,467
Athena disguises him as an old beggar,

130
00:04:29,467 --> 00:04:30,900
so he can scout his own house.

131
00:04:31,233 --> 00:04:33,933
His old dog, Argos, knows him at once,

132
00:04:33,933 --> 00:04:36,500
wags his tail one last time, and dies.

133
00:04:36,833 --> 00:04:39,100
His old nurse knows him by a scar on his leg.

134
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:41,633
Then Penelope sets a test.

135
00:04:41,967 --> 00:04:44,267
Whoever strings Odysseus's old bow

136
00:04:44,267 --> 00:04:46,733
and shoots an arrow through twelve axes

137
00:04:46,733 --> 00:04:48,000
will be her husband.

138
00:04:48,333 --> 00:04:49,600
The suitors try and fail.

139
00:04:49,933 --> 00:04:52,267
The beggar strings it as easily as a lyre,

140
00:04:52,267 --> 00:04:53,767
and shoots through all twelve.

141
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:55,533
Then the rags come off,

142
00:04:55,533 --> 00:04:57,800
and the suitors' very long party is over.

143
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:00,467
Penelope still isn't sure,

144
00:05:00,467 --> 00:05:02,533
so she asks a servant to move their bed.

145
00:05:02,867 --> 00:05:05,700
Odysseus is outraged: nobody can move that bed.

146
00:05:06,033 --> 00:05:07,867
One of its posts is an olive tree,

147
00:05:07,867 --> 00:05:09,167
still rooted in the ground.

148
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:11,300
Only the real Odysseus would know that.

149
00:05:11,633 --> 00:05:13,500
She has out-tricked the master trickster.

150
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:16,700
When the suitors' families come for revenge,

151
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:18,867
Athena makes both sides swear peace.

152
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:20,300
The poem ends there.

153
00:05:20,633 --> 00:05:21,700
The story doesn't.

154
00:05:23,533 --> 00:05:24,900
It moved into our language.

155
00:05:25,233 --> 00:05:27,933
A long, hard journey is an odyssey,

156
00:05:27,933 --> 00:05:31,000
a meaning English has used since the late 1800s.

157
00:05:31,333 --> 00:05:33,367
Stuck between two dangers?

158
00:05:33,367 --> 00:05:35,400
You're between Scylla and Charybdis.

159
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:39,300
In 2017, Emily Wilson published

160
00:05:39,300 --> 00:05:41,333
the first English translation of the poem

161
00:05:41,333 --> 00:05:42,000
by a woman.

162
00:05:42,333 --> 00:05:45,067
Her first line: Tell me about a complicated man.

163
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:47,067
The Greek word means, roughly,

164
00:05:47,067 --> 00:05:48,400
a man of many turns.

165
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:51,367
This summer, Christopher Nolan's film

166
00:05:51,367 --> 00:05:54,200
took in about 1.77 billion dollars.

167
00:05:54,533 --> 00:05:57,067
And by mid-July, before it even opened,

168
00:05:57,067 --> 00:05:58,667
US print sales of the poem

169
00:05:58,667 --> 00:06:00,933
were up 76 percent year to date.

170
00:06:01,267 --> 00:06:03,433
Just know the film skips the Nobody trick,

171
00:06:03,433 --> 00:06:06,400
and on screen, Athena is the only god.

172
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:08,833
Quick game: real or fake?

173
00:06:09,167 --> 00:06:11,333
One: the Trojan Horse is in the Odyssey.

174
00:06:11,667 --> 00:06:14,200
Two: Odysseus's dog recognizes him

175
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:15,133
after twenty years.

176
00:06:15,467 --> 00:06:18,467
Three: Homer says the Cyclops has one eye

177
00:06:18,467 --> 00:06:19,667
in the middle of his forehead.

178
00:06:22,667 --> 00:06:23,800
The fake is number three.

179
00:06:24,133 --> 00:06:26,400
Homer only mentions his eye, singular,

180
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:27,667
and never says where it is.

181
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:29,767
The forehead comes from Hesiod,

182
00:06:29,767 --> 00:06:31,433
describing different Cyclopes.

183
00:06:31,767 --> 00:06:32,800
The horse is real.

184
00:06:33,133 --> 00:06:35,100
It's in the Odyssey three times,

185
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:36,333
and never in the Iliad.

186
00:06:37,333 --> 00:06:39,233
So why did a trip home take ten years?

187
00:06:39,567 --> 00:06:41,567
One boast, one angry sea god,

188
00:06:41,567 --> 00:06:42,933
and some very long visits.

189
00:06:43,267 --> 00:06:45,033
Next time you post where you are,

190
00:06:45,033 --> 00:06:46,133
think of Odysseus.

191
00:06:47,133 --> 00:06:48,967
But why was he at Troy in the first place?

192
00:06:49,300 --> 00:06:50,367
Blame a golden apple.

193
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:52,633
That's a later myth, and our next video.

194
00:06:53,633 --> 00:06:54,800
Thanks for watching.

195
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:56,500
Subscribe so you don't miss it,

196
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:57,633
and see you next time.

